.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt USBIP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm usbip
.Nd attach USB devices exported over the network
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm list
.Fl r Ar host
.Op Fl t Ar port
.Nm
.Cm attach
.Fl r Ar host
.Fl b Ar busid
.Op Fl t Ar port
.Nm
.Cm detach
.Fl p Ar port
.Nm
.Cm port
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility attaches USB devices exported by a remote USB/IP server, so that
they appear on the local USB bus and can be used by ordinary drivers.
It is the userland half of
.Xr vhci 4 ,
which must be loaded.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width "detach"
.It Cm list
List the devices
.Ar host
is willing to export.
This does not need
.Xr vhci 4
and does not change anything.
.It Cm attach
Import the device named by
.Ar busid
from
.Ar host
and attach it to a free port.
The
.Ar busid
is one of the identifiers reported by
.Cm list .
On success the port it was attached to is printed.
.It Cm detach
Detach the device on the given
.Ar port ,
as though it had been unplugged.
.It Cm port
List the ports that currently have a device attached.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-b busid"
.It Fl r Ar host
The server to talk to.
.It Fl b Ar busid
The device to import, as reported by
.Cm list .
.It Fl t Ar port
The TCP port the server listens on.
The default is 3240.
.It Fl p Ar port
The local
.Xr vhci 4
port number, as reported by
.Cm port .
.El
.Pp
Attaching and detaching require root privileges;
.Cm list
does not.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.Nm
performs the USB/IP handshake itself and then hands the connected socket
to
.Xr vhci 4 ,
which owns it from that point on.
.Nm
does not stay running: there is no daemon to keep alive, and killing the
shell it was run from does not disturb an attached device.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
List what a server offers and attach one of its devices:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload vhci
$ usbip list -r server.example.org
Exportable USB devices on server.example.org:
  1-1            0781:5567  High Speed (480 Mbit/s)
                  class 00/00/00  conf 1  ifaces 1
                  if0  class 08/06/50
# usbip attach -r server.example.org -b 1-1
imported 1-1: 0781:5567 (High Speed (480 Mbit/s)), devid 0x10002
attached to port 0
.Ed
.Pp
The device is now visible to
.Xr usbconfig 8
and to class drivers.
When finished:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# usbip detach -p 0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr usb 4 ,
.Xr vhci 4 ,
.Xr usbconfig 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility was written in 2026.
It speaks the same protocol as the Linux tool of the same name but
shares no code with it.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Atsushi Furuta Aq Mt furuta@furuta.bsdclub.org
.Sh CAVEATS
The connection carries USB traffic in the clear and is not
authenticated.
Anything attached over an untrusted network should be treated as
untrusted, both for what it can see and for what it presents itself as.
